{"causes": ["The successful First Crusade (1096-1099) inspired subsequent expeditions, including the Crusade of 1101, which Anselm IV would lead.", "Pope Urban II's call for continued support for the Latin East after the capture of Jerusalem motivated many, including Anselm IV, to participate in new crusading efforts.", "The ongoing Gregorian Reform movement fostered a proactive and militant papacy, encouraging high-ranking clergy like Anselm IV to take direct leadership roles in church-sanctioned military ventures."], "effects": ["Anselm IV's death contributed to the disastrous failure of the Lombard contingent in the Crusade of 1101, severely depleting European manpower and resources intended for the Crusader states.", "His leadership and demise during the crusade underscored the significant risks and direct involvement of high-ranking ecclesiastical figures in military campaigns of the period.", "The catastrophic defeat of the Crusade of 1101, partly due to the disorganization of forces like Anselm's, prompted a re-evaluation of crusading strategies and logistics for subsequent expeditions."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "anselm-iv", "sourceName": "Anselm IV", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "urban-ii", "targetName": "Pope Urban II", "context": "Anselm IV was a close friend and staunch supporter of Pope Urban II, aligning his archdiocese with papal reform efforts."}, {"sourceSlug": "crusade-of-1101", "sourceName": "Crusade of 1101", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "anselm-iv", "targetName": "Anselm IV", "context": "Anselm IV was a prominent leader in the Crusade of 1101, commanding its large Lombard contingent."}, {"sourceSlug": "anselm-iv", "sourceName": "Anselm IV", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "merzifon-turkey", "targetName": "Merzifon, Turkey", "context": "Anselm IV died near Merzifon in Anatolia during a Seljuk ambush on September 30, 1101."}, {"sourceSlug": "anselm-iv", "sourceName": "Anselm IV", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "gregorian-reform", "targetName": "Gregorian Reform", "context": "Anselm IV actively supported and implemented the principles of the Gregorian Reform within the Archdiocese of Milan."}, {"sourceSlug": "anselm-iv", "sourceName": "Anselm IV", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "lombard-contingent-1101", "targetName": "Lombard Contingent (1101)", "context": "Anselm IV's leadership directly organized and commanded the large Lombard contingent of the Crusade of 1101."}], "places": [{"name": "Milan, Italy", "role": "Archiepiscopal See"}, {"name": "Merzifon, Turkey", "role": "Place of Death"}, {"name": "Constantinople, Byzantine Empire", "role": "Crusader Rally Point"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Anselm IV", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "anselm-iv"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1152589", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anselm_IV_(archbishop_of_Milan)"], "tags": ["Religious Figures", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Anselm IV's leadership of the Lombard contingent in the Crusade of 1101 and his subsequent death during its catastrophic failure highlighted the profound challenges of early crusading and the direct, often fatal, involvement of high-ranking clergy. His actions and demise contributed significantly to the strategic lessons learned from the disastrous 1101 expedition, influencing future crusading efforts.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T14:52:23Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39267311611", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Anselm IV was the Archbishop of Milan from 3 November 1097 to his death on 30 September 1101. He was a close friend of Pope Urban II and prominent in the Crusade of 1101, whose Lombard contingent he led and on which he died.", "newValue": "Anselm IV, Archbishop of Milan from November 3, 1097, until his death on September 30, 1101, emerged as a pivotal ecclesiastical and military leader in the wake of the First Crusade. A fervent supporter of papal reform and a close confidant of Pope Urban II, Anselm's career was defined by his commitment to the Gregorian Reforms and, crucially, his leadership of the formidable Lombard contingent during the ill-fated Crusade of 1101. His participation underscored the profound entanglement of spiritual authority with military action during the high Middle Ages, particularly in northern Italy.

Elevated to the archiepiscopal see of Milan, a strategically vital and often contentious position, Anselm IV inherited a region deeply affected by the Investiture Controversy and the ongoing struggle between papal and imperial factions. He actively worked to consolidate papal authority within his archdiocese, aligning Milan with Rome's reforming agenda. His most significant undertaking, however, was his command of the Lombard forces—estimated at 50,000 men, women, and children—who departed from Milan in September 1100, embarking on a perilous overland journey through the Balkans and Anatolia. This contingent, known for its size and initial disorganization, aimed to reinforce the newly established Crusader states in the Levant.

Anselm IV's death near Merzifon in Anatolia on September 30, 1101, during a catastrophic ambush by Seljuk forces, marked a tragic end to the Lombard Crusade and significantly impacted the morale of the remaining crusader contingents. His demise, alongside thousands of his followers, served as a stark reminder of the immense logistical and military challenges faced by these early expeditions. The failure of the 1101 Crusade, despite its scale, left the Crusader states in a precarious position and highlighted the need for more coordinated and disciplined military efforts, influencing future crusading strategies."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-19T14:52:23Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39267311611", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1100, Bovisio-Masciago"}, {"title": "Spiritual seeking and religious community formation", "type": "Idea", "year": "1100, Bovisio-Masciago"}], "newValue": ["The successful First Crusade (1096-1099) inspired subsequent expeditions, including the Crusade of 1101, which Anselm IV would lead.", "Pope Urban II's call for continued support for the Latin East after the capture of Jerusalem motivated many, including Anselm IV, to participate in new crusading efforts.", "The ongoing Gregorian Reform movement fostered a proactive and militant papacy, encouraging high-ranking clergy like Anselm IV to take direct leadership roles in church-sanctioned military ventures."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-19T14:52:23Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39267311611", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1101-10-07"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1101-10-07"}], "newValue": ["Anselm IV's death contribut